  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 256 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSaturday evening sees upper level system digging into the Great \nPlains and progressing to the east. Surface mass response within \nthe left exit region of the jet streak lead to low pressure \nforming within the midwest during the day on Saturday and slowly \ndeepening as it pushes to the east, dragging a cold front with it \nthat approaches the CWA by late Sunday afternoon to evening. With \nPWATs within guidance at around 1.25\", enough moisture should be \npresent to squeeze some rainfall out of this system as it quickly \nmoves through.\n\nGiven we are approaching CAM territory and the system has some \ndecent shear associated with it, a quick analysis of the severe \npotential is warranted. While shear profiles are impressive thanks\nto potent upper level jet streak and veering winds from the \nsurface to aloft, the thermodynamics look to be the limiting \nfactor. Given how quickly this follows the current system \nimpacting the area, the system struggles to return enough warmth \nand moisture from the Gulf to have much of an impact. Can't rule \nout some rumbles of thunder at this time, but with poor lapse \nrates and near zero instability the cold front will be doing \nnearly all the work to generate any lift. Not anticipating severe \nweather at this time, but worth keeping an eye on.\n\nMoving into the week, cold front that pushes through the area \nisn't the most potent given a more southerly source region for the\nair mass, so highs quickly come back to the upper 50s to 60s on \nMonday. Ensembles have high confidence in seeing ridging build in \naloft by the midweek, keeping conditions dry and high temperatures\na couple of degrees above average.",
